  "step_2": "The verbal report states: 'B war die einzige Option einen Verlust zu umgehen.' This translates to: 'B was the only option to avoid a loss.' The individual focuses on the possibility of avoiding a loss entirely, which is possible in Lottery B (with 50% probability, the outcome is 0 Euros), whereas Lottery A guarantees a loss of 500 Euros. The individual considers the presence of a zero-loss outcome in B and views this as the sole way to escape a loss. The justification centers on loss avoidance, specifically the existence of a chance (even if not certain) to have no loss. The individual does not mention probabilities of different loss sizes, distribution shapes, or skewness. There is no reference to the overall shape of the distribution or comparative assessment of how probabilities are allocated across favorable vs. unfavorable outcomes beyond the binary possibility of avoiding loss altogether.",
  "step_3": "The decision reason relies on assessing skewness—specifically favoring lotteries that are more right-skewed (i.e., with higher probability on unfavorable outcomes). However, the individual’s reasoning focuses exclusively on the possibility of avoiding a loss altogether, which is a feature of B (50% chance of 0 Euros). This reasoning aligns more with a 'hope for zero loss' or 'best-case focus' heuristic rather than an evaluation of skewness. Skewness would require attention to the full distribution shape and the relative weighting of outcomes, especially the concentration of probability on bad vs. good outcomes. The individual does not engage with these aspects. Moreover, Lottery B is not clearly more right-skewed; in fact, it has a 50% chance of a favorable outcome (no loss), which contradicts the definition of right-skew (which would imply concentration on bad outcomes). Therefore, the information considered by the individual (presence of a no-loss outcome) does not match the information required by the decision reason (distribution skewness). Similarly, the justification (avoiding loss) does not align with the reason’s rule (prefer more right-skewed). Thus, there is no evidence the individual used skewness as a basis for choice.",
