  "step_2": "The verbal report is in German and translates to: 'B has the greater chance to avoid a loss.' The individual focuses on the probability of avoiding a loss entirely, noting that Lottery B offers a 75% chance of 0 Euros (no loss), compared to Lottery A, which offers only a 50% chance of 0 Euros. The justification centers on the likelihood of escaping any loss, implying that this was a key factor in the decision. The individual does not mention skewness, distribution shape, or comparative evaluation of outcome probabilities beyond the chance to avoid loss. The reasoning appears to be based on the probability of the best possible outcome (0 loss), not on the overall skewness of the distribution.",
  "step_3": "The decision reason focuses on skewness, particularly favoring more right-skewed lotteries (those with higher probability on unfavorable outcomes). In this context, Lottery A is more right-skewed in the loss domain (50% chance of loss vs. 25% in B), so the reason would prefer A. However, the individual explicitly favors B because it has a higher chance of avoiding loss — that is, a higher probability of the most favorable outcome. This reasoning aligns with a 'maximize chance of best outcome' heuristic, not skewness-based reasoning. The information considered by the individual (probability of avoiding loss) is only partially overlapping with skewness (which requires full distribution assessment). The justification does not reflect a comparison of distribution shapes or skewness. Therefore, there is no evidence that the individual used skewness as a decision reason. On the contrary, the reasoning contradicts the skewness reason by favoring the less right-skewed option.",
